{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdY92svzDKcaP3UupXFc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Spending on agentic coding tools has grown 10x since the start of the year and now equals about 2% of total engineering cost, according to a Jellyfish report based on use by 276,000 of its customers. Jellyfish chief scientist Nicholas Arcolano told TechCrunch that 2% is still a small share, but the speed of growth is something most CFOs didn't plan for. A line item that small is unlikely to draw scrutiny on its size, so the pressure comes from its trajectory and from the question of what it returns.\n\nThat question is hard to answer today. TechCrunch's article says usage among venture-backed firms is driving token spend, while most of the Fortune 500 are not seeing major gains from deploying AI. A finance team looking at a rapidly growing bill without a clear view of output has little to base a decision on.\n\nAnthropic's reporting tools, announced Wednesday, are aimed at that gap. For a chosen date range, a manager can see what a team produced, what it cost, and where its workflows broke down, and the reports include recommendations for improving results. Pooled group budgets let teams manage token spending together. The inputs are internal analytics and employee surveys, so part of the picture depends on what staff report. Whether that is enough to settle an ROI argument with a finance department is not something the article tests, and none of the figures it cites show how much value any customer actually gets.\n\nWhat the tools do provide is a record of cost next to output, organized by team and period, which is the starting point for deciding whether a 10x increase in spend is justified."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1099,"cache_read_input_tokens":4277,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_token